// The `prelude.env` sub-module: project-scoped environment access. These are host primitives — the
// runtime registers their implementations, bound to the project's `env_entries` store, when it boots (the
// pure built-ins in `prelude.ktr` need no such binding). They are called qualified as `env.get_secret`
// / `env.get_or`.
//
// TWO READERS, one per side of the secret / non-secret split, and that split is the whole shape of the
// module. A SECRET entry is read one key at a time through `get_secret`, whose result is a
// `string of private` (tainted secret — it must not cross a user-facing boundary such as a run result,
// only a sink like an http header or an FFI call), and a missing one is an anticipated CONFIGURATION
// failure worth routing, so it throws. A NON-SECRET entry is read through `get_or`, which is TOTAL: an
// unset key is not an error at all, it is a knob nobody turned, and the caller already holds the value it
// means — the @fallback@ it had to write anyway.
//
// THE TOTAL READER IS THE ONLY NON-SECRET READER, deliberately. A `null`-answering `get` and a
// whole-record `get_all` both existed, and every real call site wrapped the first in the same two-arm
// match while nothing at all called the second. A required-config CHECK is written as
// `get_or(key = ..., fallback = "") == ""` — which reads exactly as it means, with one thing to know:
// that check cannot tell an entry SET to the empty string from an unset one. Both are "nothing
// configured", which is the reading a check wants; a program that needs the distinction is asking a
// question the env store does not answer, and should carry the flag as its own non-empty value.

@"No secret env entry is set under @key@. Thrown by `get_secret` — catch it to fall back in-program
(`use handler { request throw(error: env.missing_secret) -> never { ... } }`, or fold it to a value with
`prelude.catch`)."
data missing_secret(@"The key that had no secret entry." key: string, message: string)

@"Read a secret env entry by key, returning its value as a private string (the value is tainted secret and
cannot flow to a user-facing boundary). A missing key throws `missing_secret` (carrying the key), so a
program can fall back for optional config; uncaught, the throw fails the run."
primitive agent get_secret(@"The secret entry's key in the project's env store." key: string) -> string of private with prelude.throw[missing_secret]

@"Read one NON-SECRET env entry by key, answering @fallback@ when no entry is set — the whole non-secret
read surface, and TOTAL by construction: an unset knob is not a failure, so there is nothing to catch and
nothing to branch on. @fallback@ is the value the program means when nobody configured one, which is a
value the caller was going to have to write regardless; putting it at the read site is what removes the
match every config line otherwise carries.

Secrets are NOT visible here — a secret entry reads through `get_secret`, which taints its answer private
precisely so that a public string can never be one. An entry SET to the empty string comes back as the
empty string, not as @fallback@: this reader distinguishes them, even though the idiomatic required-config
check (`get_or(key = ..., fallback = \"\") == \"\"`) deliberately does not.

For a NUMERIC knob, parse the answer where it is read —
`string.to_integer(value = string.trim(value = env.get_or(key = ..., fallback = \"\")))` and a `match` for
the `null` — because the fallback for a knob that is set to something unparseable is a judgement the
program owns, not one a reader should make silently."
primitive agent get_or(
  @"The non-secret entry's key in the project's env store." key: string,
  @"The value when no entry is set under @key@." fallback: string,
) -> string
